Comment (internal/service/llm/anthropic.go:86):
**[Medium] Structured-output schema is passed through verbatim, but the Go SDK does not strip unsupported JSON-Schema keywords.**

Anthropic structured outputs do **not** support `minimum`/`maximum`/`minLength`/`maxLength`/`multipleOf` and similar constraints. The Python and TypeScript SDKs silently *remove* these from the schema before sending and validate them client-side; the Go SDK does **not**. So if a downstream (Phase 2/3) caller passes a schema containing any of those keywords, the live `/v1/messages` request will likely return **400 → `ErrInvalidOutput`** (non-retryable), and that structured call will fail permanently.

The design comment below (line 134) frames this as the API merely "not enforcing" the constraints — but the real risk is the API *rejecting* them, which is precisely why the other SDKs strip them.

Suggestion: strip unsupported keywords from `req.Schema` before building `JSONOutputFormatParam` (a small recursive walk dropping the unsupported keys), or document loudly that callers must pre-sanitize. Either way, verify the behavior against the real API — see the related note on the passthrough test, which currently masks this.
